## Question 24

*MCQ · 3 marks*

Which header file must be included to use dynamic memory allocation functions in C?

- **(A)** 

  ![option A](../assets/AN-Programming-in-C_p13_fe1f3baf82.jpeg)

- **(B)** 

  ![option B](../assets/AN-Programming-in-C_p13_4a81bb19d4.jpeg)

- **(C)** 

  ![option C](../assets/AN-Programming-in-C_p13_e209520678.jpeg)

- **(D)** 

  ![option D](../assets/AN-Programming-in-C_p13_d44fdd8e51.jpeg)


### Answer

**(B)** 

### Explanation

#### The functions in question

C's dynamic memory allocation functions are:

```c
void *malloc(size_t size);
void *calloc(size_t n, size_t size);
void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t size);
void  free(void *ptr);
```

All four are declared in **`<stdlib.h>`** ("standard library"), so option **(B)** is correct.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>            /* required for malloc / free */

int main(void) {
    int *arr = malloc(5 * sizeof(int));
    if (arr == NULL) return 1;
    arr[0] = 42;
    printf("%d\n", arr[0]);
    free(arr);
    return 0;
}
```

`<stdlib.h>` is also where `exit`, `atoi`, `strtol`, `rand`, `qsort`, `abs` and the `size_t`/`NULL` definitions live - the general-purpose utilities that are not I/O and not string handling.

#### Why the header matters, not just the linker

The functions live in the C runtime library, so the program *links* either way. But without the declaration, a pre-C99 compiler assumes `malloc` returns `int`, and truncating a 64-bit pointer into an `int` corrupts it - a crash that looks unrelated to the missing `#include`. In C99 and later an implicit declaration is an outright error. So omitting `<stdlib.h>` is a real bug, not a style issue.

#### The misconception in each wrong option

- **(A) `<stdio.h>`** - standard **I/O**: `printf`, `scanf`, `fopen`, `FILE`, `getchar`. Because almost every example program starts with `#include <stdio.h>`, learners assume it supplies everything. It does define `NULL` and `size_t`, which is why forgetting `<stdlib.h>` sometimes *almost* compiles.
- **(C) `<string.h>`** - byte/string handling: `strlen`, `strcpy`, `strcmp`, `memcpy`, `memset`. The word "memory" in `memcpy`/`memset` is the trap here: those functions *operate on* memory you already own; they never obtain it. Allocation and manipulation are different headers.
- **(D) `<ctype.h>`** - single-character classification: `isalpha`, `isdigit`, `toupper`, `tolower`. Nothing to do with memory at all; it is included as a plausible-looking distractor.

The deciding fact: `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc` and `free` are declared in `<stdlib.h>`.

✅ **Verified** — matches the answer key embedded in the paper.

